{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C1jW6ixFzc36am1gx8jHgE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SktAtZtkfhJaJz68aZBCPv","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7818,"cache_read_input_tokens":33060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7818,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H1Bm84fB9pNCfnJvMUKETy","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb","uuid":"49e75f15-13f9-471c-8f8b-e199d4cf3d0d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SktAtZtkfhJaJz68aZBCPv","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H1Bm84fB9pNCfnJvMUKETy","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb","uuid":"013728db-144c-44b3-b55c-4b62e9092de0","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:11:42.827Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a1d1674ff8b98c40d","tool_use_id":"toolu_01H1Bm84fB9pNCfnJvMUKETy","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":41600,"tool_uses":5,"duration_ms":93278},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b9634445-5c0e-4b98-8939-3123b6d448c6","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HmXd2Sko6VrGAvALVE6DKg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PKWTPt2WwaSVGYzcUzZkbo","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## Reviewer 2: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper formalizes \"ordinal generation depth estimation\" as a classification task and constructs a Depth Degradation Atlas mapping how depth signal varies across model scale, architecture, and decoding strategy. The core novelty claim is the task formalization itself and the systematic empirical characterization, rather than a new method. The paper is honest about its negative findings (14B signal decay, API mechanism specificity, competitive baselines) but this honesty simultaneously undermines the significance of the contribution.\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: The task formalization of ordinal depth estimation is genuinely new. Prior work on model collapse characterizes degradation trajectories given known depth; this paper inverts the question to estimation from text alone. This conceptual reframing, while simple, opens a distinct research direction.\n- S2: The paper is unusually candid about its own limitations. The Abstract and Introduction do not oversell; contribution 4 explicitly states that existing detectors provide competitive baselines. Section 8 explicitly disclaims theoretical novelty. Section 9.2 enumerates five practical scope constraints. This level of intellectual honesty is rare and commendable.\n- S3: The three-way interaction (scale x architecture x strategy) is a useful organizational framework. Prior model collapse studies examine these factors in isolation; the Atlas provides a joint characterization. The finding that feature rankings diverge across scales (1/4 overlap between 1B and 14B top features) is a concrete, non-obvious empirical insight.\n- S4: The API model experiment (Section 5.2) and the zero cross-method transfer result are valuable negative findings that constrain the generality of model collapse phenomena in ways the community has not previously documented.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1 [Major] [idea-level]: **Insufficient novelty for a top venue.** The paper proposes no new method, no new model, no new loss function, and no new theoretical result. The task formalization is a relabeling of existing binary detection into ordinal bins. The features (19 statistical features including perplexity statistics, n-gram repetition, type-token ratio) are entirely standard text quality metrics. The classifiers (Random Forest, Logistic Regression, CORAL) are off-the-shelf. The primary \"contribution\" is running these standard tools across a grid of configurations and reporting results. For EMNLP Main, even with a \"New Missions\" framing, the intellectual contribution must go beyond systematic application of known tools to a new label set. **Major because:** the novelty deficit is structural, not fixable by adding experiments. The paper would need a genuinely new detection method or a theoretical framework with predictive power to reach the novelty bar.\n\n- W2 [Major] [idea-level]: **The negative results, while honest, collectively undermine the paper's own relevance.** The paper's key findings are: (a) depth estimation fails at 14B scale (52%, EDD=1), (b) it fails for API models (38%), (c) existing detectors achieve 63% without specialized features, (d) seed sensitivity reaches 6.8pp, (e) wild-data cross-model agreement is only \"fair\" (kappa=0.31). Taken together, these findings suggest the phenomenon being studied (ordinal depth signal from statistical features) is narrow: it works primarily for small (1B) base models fine-tuned with LoRA using specific decoding strategies. The paper acknowledges this but still frames the work as a \"foundation for future work.\" A reviewer must ask: foundation for what? If the signal vanishes at practical scales and does not transfer across mechanisms, the research direction itself appears to be a dead end rather than a foundation. **Major because:** this is not a presentation issue but a fundamental question about whether the studied phenomenon has sufficient scope to warrant a full paper at a top venue.\n\n- W3 [Minor] [idea-level]: The EDD metric, presented as a contribution, is a straightforward threshold on pairwise AUC. Defining \"the maximum depth d such that AUC(d, d+1) >= T\" is a one-line definition, not a methodological contribution. The paper lists it prominently but it adds minimal intellectual content.\n\n- W4 [Minor] [presentation-level]: **Related work coverage is thin.** The reference list contains only 21 entries. Several important lines of work are missing: (1) DetectGPT (Mitchell et al., 2023) is the foundational zero-shot detection method and is not cited, though Fast-DetectGPT is; (2) DNA-GPT (Yang et al., 2023) and related re-generation based detection methods that also exploit distributional properties; (3) the broader watermarking literature beyond Kirchenbauer (e.g., Christ et al., 2024; Zhao et al., 2024); (4) more recent model collapse theoretical work (e.g., Seddik et al., 2024; Feng et al., 2024 on scaling law interactions with data contamination); (5) multi-class source attribution work beyond Uchendu (e.g., Li et al., 2024 on fine-grained attribution). The Related Work section is only ~0.75 pages and reads more as a brief survey than a thorough positioning.\n\n- W5 [Minor] [presentation-level]: **Contribution 2 (\"Depth Degradation Atlas\") conflates the benchmark construction with the empirical findings.** The Atlas is both the dataset and the analysis, making it unclear what the standalone reusable artifact is. If the Atlas is the benchmark, it should be described with dataset statistics and release plans. If it is the empirical findings, it is not an artifact but a set of observations. This ambiguity weakens the contribution framing.\n\n- W6 [Minor] [idea-level]: The paper does not adequately address why ordinal depth estimation matters given that binary detection (d0 vs d1+) already works well (AUC 0.88 even at 14B). The motivation in the Introduction argues for \"graduated filtering strategies that preserve useful synthetic content while removing deeply degraded text,\" but the experiments show that distinguishing d1 from d2 from d3 is precisely where the method fails at practical scales. The motivating use case thus does not survive contact with the paper's own results.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: Given that the depth signal is mechanism-specific (LoRA only), scale-dependent (1B only), and seed-sensitive (up to 6.8pp), what concrete downstream application do you envision for ordinal depth estimation? The graduated filtering motivation requires the method to work on real web data with unknown provenance, which Section 7 shows it cannot.\n- Q2: Have you considered neural classifiers (e.g., fine-tuned BERT/RoBERTa on the 19 features or raw text) as baselines? The paper only evaluates RF, LR, and CORAL. A neural baseline would help assess whether the feature engineering is the bottleneck or the task itself is inherently difficult at adjacent depths.\n- Q3: The 14B experiment uses only Qwen2.5-14B. How do you justify generalizing to \"14B scale\" from a single model family? LLaMA-2-13B or Mistral-7B (closer to 14B with different architectures) would strengthen or weaken this claim substantially.\n\n### Novelty Assessment\nThe genuinely new element is the conceptual reframing: treating recursive generation depth as an ordinal estimation problem rather than a binary detection problem. This is a valid intellectual contribution but a thin one. The task definition is a one-paragraph formalization. Everything downstream (features, classifiers, evaluation metrics) is standard. The Atlas concept (systematic grid evaluation) is a useful organizational choice but not a methodological innovation. The negative findings (14B decay, API specificity) are empirically valuable but are observations, not contributions in the traditional sense. The paper reads as a thorough empirical investigation using existing tools applied to a modestly new question. For a workshop paper or Findings, this could suffice. For EMNLP Main, the novelty bar requires either a new method that advances the state of the art or theoretical insights with predictive power, neither of which this paper provides.\n\n### Related Work Gap Analysis\nThe related work has structural gaps. The binary detection literature is under-covered: DetectGPT (Mitchell et al., 2023) is missing entirely, and the section does not discuss the spectrum of detection methods (perturbation-based, likelihood-based, watermark-based, trained classifiers) in enough detail to position the ordinal extension. The model collapse section covers the three foundational papers (Shumailov, Dohmatob, Gerstgrasser) adequately but misses more recent theoretical refinements. The data curation section is cursory (two references). The authorship attribution section cites only Uchendu 2020, missing substantial recent progress. With 21 total references for a paper that spans model collapse, text detection, data curation, and ordinal classification, the citation density is well below EMNLP norms (typically 40-60 references for an empirical study of this scope). The positioning is honest but incomplete.\n\n### Story Consistency Check\nThe narrative arc is internally consistent, which is the paper's structural strength. The Abstract announces boundary conditions and negative findings; the Introduction frames the work as characterization rather than solution; the experiments deliver on exactly the announced scope; the Discussion and Limitations enumerate constraints that match the data. There is no overclaiming.\n\nHowever, there is a subtle inconsistency between the Introduction's motivating question and the paper's actual findings. The Introduction asks \"given text alone, can we estimate how many recursive cycles it has undergone?\" and motivates this with \"depth-aware curation.\" The paper's own results answer: \"only under narrow conditions (1B base models, LoRA fine-tuning, known decoding strategy), and even then with limited precision.\" The Conclusion handles this gracefully by reframing toward \"characterizing scope and limits,\" but the opening motivation and the closing reality are in tension. A reader following the Introduction's promise of a practical depth estimation framework will be disappointed by what the experiments actually show.\n\nOne additional inconsistency: Contribution 1 claims \"task formalization\" as a standalone contribution, but the task is defined in a single paragraph (Section 3.1). Calling a one-paragraph problem definition a \"contribution\" inflates the term.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Justification: Two Major weaknesses identified: (1) insufficient novelty for a top venue (no new method, theory, or non-obvious technical insight beyond systematic grid evaluation with standard tools), and (2) the paper's own negative results collectively undermine the practical scope of the phenomenon being studied, raising questions about whether the research direction has sufficient impact. Per calibration rules, 2 Major issues cap the score at Borderline Accept (4), but the combination of thin novelty with self-undermining results pushes below that threshold to Borderline Reject (3). The paper is well-written and honest, which prevents a score of 2, but the contribution does not meet the bar for EMNLP Main.","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260518T162616","overall_score":"borderline reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":695,"cache_read_input_tokens":40878,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":695,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01H1Bm84fB9pNCfnJvMUKETy","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb","uuid":"7bada800-22cb-469c-beb3-ef1cf1d71140"}
