{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a9a4236c393ee3258","tool_use_id":"toolu_012kcX7EDrsV5N6eWMffKS44","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":41578,"tool_uses":5,"duration_ms":110082},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"05e276cd-049a-435b-84c0-cc726cf764b3","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DLEc28sYsTigLihBwLgnpU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HFn9t9t4Z3nr48zhqbUYAn","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## Reviewer 1: Method + Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThis paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation as a four-class classification task (depths 0-3) and constructs a \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" spanning 3 model families, 3 decoding strategies, and two scales (1B and 14B). Using 19 statistical features extracted via held-out reference models and Random Forest / logistic regression classifiers, the paper systematically maps where depth estimation succeeds (1B base models with LoRA, 70-85% accuracy) and where it fails (14B scale: 52%, API fine-tuning: 38%, cross-method transfer: 27%). The paper honestly reports boundary conditions including seed sensitivity (up to 6.8pp), competitive non-specialized baselines (Binoculars: 63%), and low wild-data cross-model agreement (kappa=0.31).\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: The experimental design is impressively comprehensive for an empirical study. The 3x3 grid at 1B scale, extension to 14B, API models, detector baselines, seed sensitivity, wild-data calibration, and bootstrap CIs cover a wide space of conditions. The paper does not cherry-pick favorable conditions.\n- S2: Honest and transparent reporting of negative results. The paper explicitly acknowledges that 14B depth estimation effectively reduces to binary detection (EDD=1), that API-based chains yield near-chance accuracy, and that Binoculars achieves 63% without specialized features. The limitations section (Section 10) lists seven concrete constraints. This level of self-awareness is rare and scientifically commendable.\n- S3: The method description is clear and reproducible. Feature extraction categories (perplexity, surprisal, lexical diversity, repetition) are enumerated, classifier configurations (RF 500 trees, LR, CORAL) are specified, the recursive chain construction procedure is well-defined, and the held-out reference model design avoids circularity. Key hyperparameters (LoRA rank 16, ~0.15% parameters at 14B) are stated.\n- S4: Bootstrap confidence intervals with CI overlap analysis (Table 1, Section 4.1) and the EDD metric at multiple thresholds (Table 2) are methodologically sound additions that allow readers to assess statistical reliability rather than relying on point estimates.\n- S5: The cross-method transfer experiment (Section 5.2, Table 5) is a well-designed ablation that tests whether depth signal is a property of recursive generation in general or specific to LoRA fine-tuning. The zero feature overlap and chance-level transfer accuracy (27%) provide clean evidence for mechanism specificity.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1 [Major] [idea-level]: **Claim generality far exceeds experimental scope at 14B scale.** The paper's title and framing promise characterization \"across scales,\" but the 14B experiment uses only a single model family (Qwen2.5-14B) with a single decoding strategy (nucleus p=0.95). This is a single data point, not a systematic characterization. The 1B Atlas uses 3 models x 3 strategies = 9 configurations; the 14B \"Atlas\" is 1x1 = 1 configuration. The paper cannot distinguish whether the 52% accuracy is a property of scale in general, of Qwen2.5 specifically, or of nucleus sampling at 14B. A second 14B model family or at least 2-3 strategy variants would be needed to claim scale-dependent signal decay as a general finding rather than a single-model observation. **Why Major:** The scale-dependent decay is positioned as a central finding (Contribution 3, Abstract, title), but the evidence is a single experimental cell. This is insufficient experimental scope for the generality of the claim.\n\n- W2 [Major] [idea-level]: **The marginal advantage over repurposed existing detectors undermines the practical motivation.** The RF classifier (78.3% averaged) outperforms Binoculars (63%) by 15pp on average, but this gap shrinks substantially in weak cells. For top-k 50, RF achieves 69.6% [66.8%, 72.6%] while Binoculars achieves 58%, and the RF CI lower bound (66.8%) is only 8.8pp above Binoculars. For 3-class accuracy (excluding the easy d0-vs-rest distinction), RF achieves 72% vs. Binoculars 55%, which is more convincing but is reported only in passing. The paper frames specialized features as providing \"additional discriminative power\" but does not adequately argue why this 15pp average gap (which shrinks to marginal significance in weak cells) justifies the complexity of training recursive chains, extracting 19 features, and training classifiers, when a zero-shot detector score binning achieves competitive performance. **Why Major:** This directly challenges the necessity claim of the specialized feature approach (Contribution 4 itself acknowledges this). Combined with the 14B and API failures, the practical case for the proposed framework is thin: it works substantially better than baselines only for 1B base models with specific LoRA fine-tuning and non-top-k strategies, a narrow deployment niche.\n\n- W3 [Minor] [idea-level]: **Seed sensitivity analysis is incomplete.** Only 2 seeds are tested, and only for 3 models (not across all 9 cells of the Atlas). The 6.8pp gap for OLMo is reported as the maximum, but without multi-seed experiments for all cells, the actual variance envelope of the entire Atlas is unknown. The paper acknowledges this (Limitation 3) but still reports all Table 1 results as point estimates with only CV-based CIs, not seed-based CIs. The CV CIs and seed CIs measure different sources of variance; conflating them underestimates total uncertainty.\n\n- W4 [Minor] [idea-level]: **Wild-data calibration (Section 7) lacks ground truth and therefore cannot distinguish classifier error from genuine depth distribution.** The cross-model disagreement (kappa=0.31) is reported as evidence that wild-data deployment is unreliable, but it could also mean that different reference models are sensitive to different aspects of depth signal. Without any ground-truth depth labels on wild data (even a small annotated subset), the section cannot conclude whether the disagreement reflects (a) fundamentally unreliable depth estimation, (b) reference-model-dependent calibration offsets, or (c) genuine heterogeneity in wild-data depth distributions. The paper correctly frames this as an open challenge but still draws conclusions about \"deployment reliability\" from cross-model agreement alone.\n\n- W5 [Minor] [presentation-level]: **The theoretical context section (Section 8) occupies ~0.75 pages but explicitly disclaims contribution status.** The three connection points (tail erosion, EDD bound, scale decay) are all qualitative matches or vacuously loose bounds (EDD upper bound 6 vs. empirical 3-4). The paper honestly states \"We include this section for theoretical context, not as a contribution,\" which raises the question of whether this space would be better used for additional experimental analysis (e.g., more 14B configurations, per-feature ablation, or deeper wild-data analysis). As written, the section adds little that a knowledgeable reader could not infer from the empirical tables.\n\n- W6 [Minor] [presentation-level]: **Table 4 (14B per-class recall) uses approximate values (~90, ~45, ~30, ~40) rather than exact numbers with CIs.** Given that the paper provides precise CIs for 1B results, the 14B results should receive the same treatment. The approximate notation undermines the quantitative rigor that the rest of the paper maintains.\n\n- W7 [Minor] [presentation-level]: **Table 9 is missing RedPajama cross-model agreement values** (shown as dashes). The text mentions RedPajama FPR results (Table 10) but does not explain why agreement metrics are absent for RedPajama. If the data was collected, it should be reported; if not, the reason should be stated.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: For the 14B experiment, why was only nucleus sampling tested? Given the strong strategy-dependent effects observed at 1B (top-k vs. nucleus vs. temperature produce 10-15pp accuracy differences), omitting strategy variation at 14B leaves open whether the weak signal is a scale effect or a scale-strategy interaction effect.\n- Q2: The cross-method transfer accuracy (27%) is near chance (25%). Was a statistical test performed to confirm it is not significantly different from random? If cross-method transfer is truly at chance, the claim should be stated more precisely.\n- Q3: For the Binoculars baseline, CORAL ordinal regression improves 4-class accuracy from 63% to 65%. Was CORAL also applied to the RF classifier? If the RF baseline (78.3%) already uses standard multiclass classification while Binoculars gets the benefit of ordinal regression, the comparison is not fully controlled.\n- Q4: The paper uses C4 as the seed corpus for all recursive chains (Limitation 1). Given the wild-data experiments on FineWeb and RedPajama, was any analysis done on whether seed corpus choice affects depth signal strength?\n- Q5: Feature importance rankings shift between 1B and 14B (only 1/4 overlap). Did you examine whether a 14B-optimized feature set (re-selected for 14B) could improve 14B accuracy, or is the 52% result already using the best available features for that scale?\n\n### Experiment Coverage Assessment\nComparing against review_context.yaml imagined experiments:\n\n**Covered:**\n1. **14B_scale_validation_worst** (plan_014): Covered in Section 5.1, Tables 3-4. Key metrics (52% 4-class acc, AUC decay, feature divergence, EDD=1) all reported. However, only nucleus strategy tested vs. the YAML's specification, reducing the scope relative to the 1B Atlas.\n2. **wild_data_calibration_worst** (plan_015): Covered in Section 7, Tables 9-10. Cross-model agreement, FPR ranges, and Cohen's kappa all reported. RedPajama agreement values missing from Table 9.\n3. **detector_ordinal_baseline_worst** (plan_018): Well covered in Section 6, Tables 7-8. Binoculars and Fast-DetectGPT both tested with binning and CORAL. Per-strategy breakdown provided.\n4. **bootstrap_ci_table1_worst** (plan_020): Covered in Section 4.1, Table 1. CI widths, overlap analysis, and cross-strategy/cross-model LOO CIs all reported.\n5. **theoretical_analysis_worst** (plan_023): Covered in Section 8, Table 11. Three connection points with honest assessment of match quality.\n6. **api_model_recursive_chains_worst** (plan_026): Covered in Section 5.2, Table 5. Feature overlap, cross-method transfer, prompted rewrite all reported.\n7. **olmo_seed43_supplementary_worst** (registry): Covered in Section 5.3, Table 6. Seed sensitivity with per-class recall and CI overlap reported.\n\n**Missing or underspecified:**\n- The YAML specifies the 14B experiment should include \"d0-d3 recursive chain\" with RF/LR classification, but the paper only reports RF results for 14B (no LR comparison at 14B, unlike 1B where both are discussed).\n- The YAML specifies \"19 feature extraction\" at 14B, but the paper does not confirm whether all 19 features were extracted at 14B or provide a 14B feature importance table (only a text description of top features).\n- Per-strategy Binoculars breakdown (Table 8) partially covers the YAML's \"per cell\" specification but only reports best/worst cells rather than the full 9-cell breakdown.\n\n**Overall:** The paper covers all 7 imagined experiment groups from the YAML, which is commendable. The main gap is the single-configuration 14B experiment, which the YAML itself also scopes narrowly (single strategy). The coverage is sufficient to support the paper's boundary-condition claims at a high level but insufficient for the scale-generality claims embedded in the title and Contribution 3.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n### Justification: Two Major weaknesses identified. W1 (single 14B configuration claimed as scale-general finding) and W2 (marginal advantage over repurposed baselines undermining practical motivation) together constrain the score to no higher than Borderline Accept (4) per calibration rules. However, the combination of these two issues is compounding: the method works well only in a narrow niche (1B, LoRA, non-top-k), and even there the advantage over zero-shot baselines is moderate. The paper's honest framing as boundary-condition characterization partially mitigates this, but the title and Contribution 3 still overclaim relative to experimental evidence. The score lands at Borderline Reject (3) rather than Borderline Accept (4) because the two Major issues jointly erode the paper's core value proposition: if depth estimation only works for 1B LoRA models with specific strategies, and existing detectors achieve 63% without any of this machinery, the empirical contribution, while thorough, has limited impact for the broader community.\n","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260518T162616","overall_score":"borderline reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":40835,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012kcX7EDrsV5N6eWMffKS44","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb","uuid":"638b8c45-d209-4aed-89e9-6e6a8c6253c5"}
