{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FMnj9tZj7e5A9cGgotzC3P","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Round 2 cross-share response submitted. Score maintained at 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\nKey points delivered:\n\n- **Q1 (novelty vs. experimental value):** Standard tools are acceptable if insights compensate, but the experimental insights here are not deep enough. The scale-dependent feature divergence (1/4 top-feature overlap between 1B and 14B) is the most informative finding but is underdeveloped.\n- **Q2 (negative results substance):** Positive results are confined to 1B LoRA with matched strategy — too narrow for EMNLP. The paper needs either broader positive results or a mechanistic explanation for the negative ones.\n- **Q3 (missing baselines):** DetectGPT would likely land between existing baselines (58-63%), further narrowing the RF advantage. DNA-GPT poses a real experimental risk — if it matches RF, the core premise collapses.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T162616/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab5d3d12ae647d120 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab5d3d12ae647d120' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 34979\ntool_uses: 4\nduration_ms: 52444</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb","uuid":"469ae7fd-7f34-4af2-aef4-7137562b0d3d","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:14:56.568Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (method + experiments) in Round 2 of a paper review cross-share. You already reviewed this paper in Round 1 and gave it 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\n## Paper\n\"Characterizing the Boundary Conditions of Recursive Generation Depth Estimation: A Systematic Empirical Study Across Scales, Strategies, and Architectures\"\n\nPDF: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T162616/worst/review_input_f5f46a62.pdf (read pages 1-6, then 7-12)\n\n## Your Round 1 Assessment (Score: 3/6)\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **14B scale experiment too narrow (Major, idea-level):** Uses only 1 model family (Qwen2.5-14B) with 1 decoding strategy (nucleus), yet claims scale-general signal decay as a central finding. Single data point, not systematic characterization.\n2. **Binoculars baseline competitiveness (Major, idea-level):** Binoculars achieves 63% with zero specialized features, narrowing RF advantage to 15pp average and marginal in weak cells.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses:** Incomplete seed analysis (2 seeds, not all cells), wild-data without ground truth, theory section 0.75 pages but disclaims contribution, approximate values Table 4, missing RedPajama in Table 9.\n\n## Reviewer 2's Round 1 Report (Novelty + Related Work, Score: 3/6)\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **Insufficient novelty (Major, idea-level):** No new method, model, loss, or predictive theory. Systematic grid evaluation using standard features (perplexity stats, n-gram repetition, type-token ratio) and off-the-shelf classifiers (RF, LR, CORAL). Task is modestly reframed binary detection relabeled as ordinal bins.\n2. **Self-undermining scope (Major, idea-level):** Paper's own negative results (14B: 52%, API: 38%, Binoculars: 63%, seed: 6.8pp, wild-data kappa=0.31) collectively demonstrate the phenomenon is too narrow (1B LoRA-only) to support practical depth-aware data curation.\n\n**Strengths:** Task reframing is genuinely new, intellectual honesty commendable, narrative internally consistent. API zero-transfer and scale-dependent feature divergence are non-trivial insights.\n\n**Related Work Gaps:** Only 21 references; missing DetectGPT (Mitchell et al., 2023), DNA-GPT, recent model collapse theory. Citation density below EMNLP norms.\n\n## Your Task (Round 2 Cross-Share)\n\nFrom your method + experiments perspective, comment on R2's findings:\n\n1. R2 says novelty is insufficient because only standard features + off-the-shelf classifiers. Does this lack of methodological novelty affect the experimental contribution value?\n2. R2 says negative results undermine the motivating use case. From your experimental analysis, do the negative results leave enough positive experimental substance for publication?\n3. R2 flags missing references (DetectGPT etc.). Would these as additional baselines change experimental conclusions?\n\nAlso state: do you want to adjust your score after seeing R2's assessment? If so, what and why?\n\nKeep response under 400 words. Output as:\n\n```\n## R1 Round 2: Cross-Share Response\n\n### Comments on R2's Findings\n[numbered responses to the 3 questions above]\n\n### Score Adjustment\n[Maintain/Adjust score, with justification]\n```","agentId":"ab5d3d12ae647d120","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Round 2 cross-share response submitted. Score maintained at 3/6 (Borderline Reject).\n\nKey points delivered:\n\n- **Q1 (novelty vs. experimental value):** Standard tools are acceptable if insights compensate, but the experimental insights here are not deep enough. The scale-dependent feature divergence (1/4 top-feature overlap between 1B and 14B) is the most informative finding but is underdeveloped.\n- **Q2 (negative results substance):** Positive results are confined to 1B LoRA with matched strategy — too narrow for EMNLP. The paper needs either broader positive results or a mechanistic explanation for the negative ones.\n- **Q3 (missing baselines):** DetectGPT would likely land between existing baselines (58-63%), further narrowing the RF advantage. DNA-GPT poses a real experimental risk — if it matches RF, the core premise collapses.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T162616/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":52444,"totalTokens":34979,"totalToolUseCount":4,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1055,"cache_read_input_tokens":33649,"output_tokens":274,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1055},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":274,"cache_read_input_tokens":33649,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1055,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1055,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":2}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a9105b7ef47a68465","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qn5Y1UcDC8jHK8JmZfvvaT","description":"R2 Round 2 cross-share response","usage":{"total_tokens":33623,"tool_uses":4,"duration_ms":50183},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"b42bf0fc-37e4-48d5-b2b3-1acdb4644085","session_id":"0fd49434-e21b-4e9e-9642-cf81f59bb6eb"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AkFhrv3WXPdYuUVNRKCvaR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SxpcwCVMj4LGPezzF2z4Ss","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R2 Round 2: Cross-Share Response\n\n### Comments on R1's Findings\n\n**1. 14B single data point and the \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" framing.**\n\nR1 is correct that this is a serious problem, and from a novelty/framing perspective it is even worse than R1 frames it. The paper's central branding — \"Depth Degradation Atlas\" — implies systematic, cartographic coverage across the scale axis. But one model family at 14B is not a map; it is a single pin on a map. The title claims \"Across Scales,\" yet the scale axis has exactly two points (1B and 14B), with the 14B point being a single configuration. This is not \"systematic\" coverage — it is an anecdote at scale. The mismatch between the ambitious framing and the thin evidence is a novelty-relevant problem: the paper's primary contribution *is* the Atlas, and if the Atlas has gaping holes, the contribution is proportionally diminished. A genuine atlas would need at least 2-3 model families at each scale point.\n\n**2. Binoculars competitiveness and contribution framing.**\n\nThis is the finding that most directly threatens the novelty claim from my perspective. The paper frames Contribution 4 (\"Baseline calibration\") as establishing that existing detectors provide competitive ordinal baselines. But from a novelty standpoint, if an off-the-shelf detector achieves 63% with zero task-specific engineering while the proposed 19-feature RF achieves 78% (Table 7), the marginal novelty of the specialized approach shrinks considerably. R1 correctly notes the gap narrows further in weak cells (top-k). The paper honestly acknowledges this, which I credit, but \"systematic characterization\" cannot fully survive when the characterization's main conclusion is that the phenomenon barely justifies specialized methods. The contribution reduces to: \"we ran existing tools on a new task and they mostly work.\" That is useful but below the novelty bar for a main venue.\n\n**3. Data completeness issues (approximate Table 4, missing RedPajama).**\n\nThese are minor individually but compound the story consistency problem. Table 4 uses approximate values (~90, ~45, ~30, ~40) rather than exact numbers, which is unusual for a paper claiming systematic rigor. Table 9 omits RedPajama agreement rates while Table 10 includes RedPajama, creating an asymmetry that makes the wild-data narrative feel incomplete. Neither issue alone changes the assessment, but together they reinforce the impression that the \"systematic\" label overpromises relative to the actual execution.\n\n### Score Adjustment\n\n**Maintain 3/6 (Borderline Reject).** R1's findings reinforce rather than contradict my Round 1 assessment. The 14B single-data-point problem deepens my concern about overclaimed scope. Binoculars' competitiveness confirms that the marginal novelty of specialized features is slim. The paper remains intellectually honest, and the task formalization has value, but the combination of (a) no new method, (b) an \"atlas\" with major gaps, and (c) a competitive zero-effort baseline leaves the contribution below the acceptance threshold. I see no reason to adjust in either direction.","project":"recursive_gen_depth_est","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260518T162616","overall_score":"3/6 Borderline Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":686,"cache_read_input_tokens":32920,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":686,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qn5Y1UcDC8jHK8JmZfvvaT","sessio